WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 167: Woodrow Wilson: The Pueblo Collapse, the Secret Stroke, and Edith's Regency (1919–1921)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.680
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our portrait of Woodrow Wilson with one of the most extraordinary constitutional crises in American history.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:28.160
[Rebecca] Defeated by political rivals and paralyzed by a massive stroke, Wilson was shielded from the world by his fiercely devoted second wife, Edith Bolling Wilson, who quietly governed the nation as America's de facto first female president.

5
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.040
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

6
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:44.000
[Rebecca] When Woodrow Wilson returned to Washington in July 1919, he marched up Capitol Hill and presented the Treaty of Versailles to the United States Senate with imperious certainty.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.040
[George] The stage is set, the destiny disclosed.

8
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:53.200
[George] It has come about by no plan of our conceiving, but by the hand of God who led us into this way.

9
00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:04.080
[George] But waiting for him in the Senate chamber was Senator Henry Cabot Lodge of Massachusetts, the aristocratic, razor-tongued Republican majority leader and chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee.

10
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:14.400
[George] Lodge held a PhD from Harvard and despised Wilson with venomous personal and intellectual contempt, sneering that Wilson was an arrogant academic fraud.

11
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.400
[George] Backslash and backslash and Lodge knew that the American people initially supported the League of Nations.

12
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.720
[George] Therefore, rather than attack the treaty head-on, Lodge used delay as a lethal political weapon.

13
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:39.840
[George] He spent two weeks reading the entire 264-page treaty aloud in committee to an empty room, followed by six weeks of public hearings where isolationists and bitter European immigrant groups testified against it.

14
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:52.880
[George] Lodge then drafted 14 reservations, a deliberate mocking swipe at Wilson's 14 points, demanding that Congress retain absolute veto power over Article X before American troops could be deployed.

15
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.240
[George] Wilson refused to compromise on a single word.

16
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] The Senate must take its medicine, Wilson declared coldly.

17
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:11.080
[George] Undertaking that grueling 8,000 mile whistlestop journey in the sweltering heat of late summer was an act of physical martyrdom for a leader whose vascular health was already crumbling.

18
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:21.240
[George] That cross-country tour was an epic journey of democratic faith, as a sick and dying president poured out his remaining physical strength to speak with ordinary citizens.

19
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.000
[George] Ignoring the desperate pleas of his personal physician, Dr.

20
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:25.720
[George] Kerry T.

21
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:34.920
[George] Grayson, who warned that another grueling journey would kill him, Wilson resolved to bypass the Senate and appeal directly to the sovereign American people.

22
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:46.600
[George] On September 3, 1919, Wilson boarded a private 7-car train for an unprecedented 8,000-mile whistle stop campaign across the American Midwest and Pacific coast.

23
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:57.240
[George] Wilson planned to deliver 37 major speeches in 29 cities over 22 days, backslash and backslash and the physical conditions aboard the train were brutal.

24
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:06.040
[George] The temperature inside the non-air conditioned cars soared over 100 degrees as the train crossed the plains of Iowa, Nebraska, and Kansas.

25
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.240
[George] Wilson suffered from blinding, continuous headaches that made his eyes ache with agony.

26
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:17.320
[George] He choked on engine smoke, was unable to digest solid food, and coughed constantly.

27
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.360
[George] Yet before colossal crowds in Columbus, St.

28
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:28.760
[George] Louis, Seattle, and San Francisco, Wilson summoned soaring, electrifying oratory, warning with terrifying prescience.

29
00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:38.040
[George] I can predict with absolute certainty that within another generation there will be another world war if the nations of the world do not concert the method by which to prevent it.

30
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.960
[George] The speeches delivered across the American West were among the most passionate and prophetic of Wilson's life.

31
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:50.080
[George] Warning that rejecting the League of Nations would inevitably result in another catastrophic world war.

32
00:03:50.080 --> 00:04:02.880
[George] His warnings in Omaha, Seattle, and San Diego proved hauntingly accurate, predicting with tragic foresight that another generation of American boys would die on European battlefields if the league was abandoned.

33
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:14.080
[George] The tragic climax of the tour occurred on the sweltering afternoon of Thursday, September 25, 1919, inside the municipal auditorium in Pueblo, Colorado.

34
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.720
[George] Over 10,000 spectators cheered wildly as Wilson took the stage.

35
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:27.440
[George] His face was grey and twitching with unbearable pain, but as he spoke, his voice took on a heartbreaking, almost biblical majesty.

36
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:43.600
[George] He spoke of the American mothers who had lost sons in France, tears streaming down his cheeks as he delivered his final public words to the American people, backslash and backslash any wish some of the men who are talking about the treaty could visit the American cemeteries in France.

37
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.360
[George] I never wished to have it said of America that she helped save the world and then turned her back upon it.

38
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.520
[George] There is one thing that the American people always rise to, and that is the greatness of a great ideal.

39
00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.840
[George] We have accepted the truth of that ideal, and we shall not turn back.

40
00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:06.480
[George] When Wilson finished and sat down, the entire auditorium rose in weeping, thunderous adoration.

41
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.200
[George] It was his masterpiece, and his final public breath as an active statesman.

42
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:22.720
[George] The sudden, devastating collapse in Pueblo marked the tragic end of Wilson's crusade, silencing the voice of American idealism and leaving the international treaty without its champion.

43
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:32.920
[George] The emotional scene in Pueblo, with tears streaming down Wilson's cheeks as he spoke of the dead soldiers in France, was the tragic climax of his public career.

44
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:38.360
[George] That evening, as the presidential train sped eastward across the plains of Kansas, Dr.

45
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.800
[George] Grayson was summoned frantically to the executive car.

46
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:52.760
[George] He found Wilson sitting on the edge of his bed, gasping for breath, his face contorted in agony, experiencing violent nausea and a partial paralysis of his facial muscles.

47
00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:57.880
[George] Grayson recognized that the president was on the verge of a catastrophic cerebral stroke.

48
00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:03.880
[George] Grayson walked into the next car, woke first Lady Edith Wilson, and declared bluntly.

49
00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:07.560
[George] We must cancel the tour immediately and return to Washington.

50
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.760
[George] If the president gives another speech, he will die.

51
00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:11.080
[George] N.

52
00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:11.400
[George] N.

53
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.760
[George] Wilson wept in his wife's arms, begging to continue.

54
00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.280
[George] I must not stop.

55
00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:21.000
[George] If I cancel the tour, Lodge will say I am a quitter and the League is dead.

56
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.040
[George] But Edith took his trembling hands and replied gently, No, Woodrow.

57
00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.760
[George] You have fought until you can fight no more.

58
00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:37.640
[George] The remaining stops were cancelled, the trains' blinds were pulled shut, and the locomotive steamed at maximum speed directly back to Washington.

59
00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:47.960
[George] That catastrophic stroke in the White House shattered the executive branch, plunging the nation into an unprecedented constitutional crisis of presidential incapacity.

60
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:56.840
[George] The silence from the second floor of the White House concealed an unprecedented constitutional vacuum that modern amendments would later seek to prevent.

61
00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:04.200
[George] Back in the White House, Wilson appeared to rest, but the vascular catastrophe inside his brain was unstoppable.

62
00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:14.920
[George] On the morning of Thursday, October 2, 1919, Edith Wilson walked into the second-floor bathroom and found the president lying crumpled and unconscious on the marble floor.

63
00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:23.040
[George] A massive blood clot had lodged in a major cerebral artery in the right hemisphere of his brain, backslash and backslash and Dr.

64
00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.520
[George] Grayson and specialist Francis X.

65
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.680
[George] Durkham rushed to the White House.

66
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.840
[George] The diagnosis was horrifying.

67
00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.400
[George] Woodrow Wilson had suffered a massive, devastating paralytic stroke.

68
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:44.720
[George] The entire left side of his body was completely paralyzed, his left arm hung useless, his left leg was immobilized, and he had lost vision in his left eye.

69
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:54.640
[George] He could speak only in slurred, feeble whispers, and his emotional stability was destroyed, bursting into uncontrollable weeping at the slightest stimulation.

70
00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.480
[George] In 1919, there was no 25th Amendment.

71
00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:05.760
[George] If the public learned that the president was an incapacitated invalid, Congress would immediately demand that Vice President Thomas R.

72
00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.440
[George] Marshall assume power.

73
00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.560
[George] Edith Wilson looked at the doctors and resolved that her husband would remain president.

74
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:24.720
[George] Edith Wilson's secret stewardship of the executive mansion was a remarkable, desperate act of wifely devotion that shielded her broken husband from political humiliation while paralyzing federal governance.

75
00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:35.200
[George] Edith Wilson's fierce guardianship of her husband's dignity preserved his life, but at the cost of freezing the executive branch during a critical period of post-war readjustment.

76
00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:41.680
[George] What followed was the most extraordinary fourteen-month deception in the history of the American executive branch.

77
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:49.200
[George] First Lady Edith Bolling Wilson instituted what she later called her stewardship, in reality, a secret regency.

78
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:58.320
[George] For over a year, no cabinet secretary, no congressman, no foreign ambassador, and no journalist was permitted to see Woodrow Wilson.

79
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:12.600
[George] Edith established a hermetic seal around the darkened second floor bedroom, backslash and backslash and all official state papers, congressional bills, diplomatic dispatches, and cabinet memoranda were handed directly to Edith.

80
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:29.880
[George] She read them in private, decided what was important enough to put before the bedridden president, and returned the documents with handwritten executive directives written in her own neat cursive, or signed by Wilson with a trembling, unreadable scrawl while Edith guided his paralyzed hand.

81
00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:40.120
[George] When Secretary of State Robert Lansing convened cabinet meetings without her permission to discuss the crisis, Edith had Wilson fire him on the spot for insubordination.

82
00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:51.800
[George] When foreign dignitaries visited, Wilson was propped up in bed with pillows, his paralyzed left side draped under heavy blankets in the shadows, while Edith coached his whispered responses.

83
00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:58.200
[George] For all practical purposes, Edith Bolling Wilson was the first female president of the United States.

84
00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:07.400
[George] The Senate's final rejection of the Treaty of Versailles sealed the tragic demise of the League in America, retreating into the fortress of postwar isolationism.

85
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:17.080
[George] The final defeat of the treaty marked the end of an era of ambitious progressive internationalism, giving way to a decade of material focus and isolationism.

86
00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:25.880
[George] Isolated in his bedroom and manipulated by his unbending pride, Wilson refused every plea from Allied friends to compromise with the Senate.

87
00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:37.960
[George] When moderate Democratic senators begged him to accept Lodge's reservations so the treaty could pass, Wilson dictated a furious letter commanding loyal Democrats to vote against the treaty with reservations.

88
00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:49.120
[George] On March 19, 1920, the United States Senate delivered its final verdict, failing to achieve the necessary two-thirds majority to ratify the Treaty of Versailles.

89
00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:56.880
[George] The United States refused to join the League of Nations, backslash and backslash N on March 4, 1921.

90
00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.160
[George] A shattered, frail Woodrow Wilson rode down Pennsylvania Avenue alongside Warren G.

91
00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:05.440
[George] Harding, a blanket draped over his withered legs.

92
00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:17.440
[George] Wilson retired to an elegant red brick townhouse on S Street in Washington, where he spent his final three years listening to scripture, reading history, and receiving visits from old comrades.

93
00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:24.960
[George] On February 3, 1924, at age 67, Woodrow Wilson closed his eyes for the last time.

94
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.440
[George] His last whispered words were I am a broken piece of machinery.

95
00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.320
[George] When the machine is broken, I am ready.

96
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:39.600
[George] He had reached for the stars of universal peace, only to be broken by human cynicism and his own unyielding pride.

97
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:46.080
[George] Yet his vision of international law would rise from the ashes of World War II to create the United Nations.

98
00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:57.600
[George] Woodrow Wilson left office a broken, paralyzed prophet, yet his vision of international collective security would be resurrected a quarter century later in the creation of the United Nations.

99
00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:08.720
[George] History would ultimately vindicate Wilson's prophetic vision, as the principles he championed in Paris became the blueprint for the modern international order after World War II.

100
00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:15.360
[Rebecca] Woodrow Wilson's tragic final months revealed the fragile human reality at the heart of executive power.

101
00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:22.640
[Rebecca] His vision of collective security outlived his defeat, becoming the moral foundation of modern international diplomacy.

102
00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:26.320
[Rebecca] Next week, we move forward into the roaring twenties with Warren G.

103
00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:38.920
[Rebecca] Harding, the genial newspaper editor from Marion, Ohio, who promised a return to normalcy after the war, only to find his administration engulfed in the legendary scandals of Teapot Dome.

104
00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.640
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

